.TP
.B skill \-KILL \-c bash
Send \fBKILL\fR to every process whose command name is exactly
\fBbash\fR. \fB\-KILL\fR and \fB\-\-signal KILL\fR are equivalent
forms.
.TP
.B skill \-\-signal HUP \-u alice
Send \fBHUP\fR to every process owned by user \fIalice\fR. Common
idiom for asking a daemon to reload its config.
.TP
.B skill \-9 \-t pts/3
\fBKILL\fR every process attached to the pseudo-terminal
\fI/dev/pts/3\fR. Hopefully not the one you are on.
.TP
.B skill \-n \-c sshd
No-op: print PIDs that \fIwould\fR be signalled, but don't actually
signal them. Useful as a dry run before committing to the real
invocation.
.TP
.B skill \-\-signal TERM \-p 1234 \-p 5678
Equivalent to \fBkill 1234 5678\fR — \fBskill\fR has no advantage
when targeting raw PIDs, but the flag-driven form is occasionally
useful in scripts where the PID list is built incrementally.
.TP
.B skill \-l
Print every supported signal name on a single line. Same output as
\fBkill \-l\fR.
.TP
.B skill \-L
Print the full signal table as eight columns of \fInumber name\fR
pairs.
